# LLM-Assisted Reviewed Test Case Generation and Risk-Based Execution for Web Forms

This project is a complete Software Quality Assurance automation framework for generating, reviewing, prioritizing, enriching, and executing web-form test cases.

It implements the proposal workflow:

1. Extract form metadata from public web forms with Selenium and BeautifulSoup.
2. Generate positive and negative test cases using a local LLM through Ollama.
3. Review and filter weak, duplicate, unrealistic, or vague cases.
4. Assign risk severity: `high`, `medium`, `low`.
5. Enrich accepted cases with precise expected results.
6. Execute final approved cases with Selenium WebDriver and pytest.
7. Produce JSON artifacts, HTML test reports, coverage reports, and audit logs.

The two supported targets are:

- ParaBank Register for Free Online Account Access
- Automation Exercise Signup / Login

The framework is intentionally Python-first because the project is an SQA automation project, not a normal CRUD web application. A frontend, backend API server, and relational database are not required by the proposal. JSON files are used as traceable QA artifacts.

---

## 1. Locked Technology Stack

| Layer | Selected technology | Version | Reason |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Language | Python | 3.13.13 | Stable Python 3.13 line, compatible with the pinned Selenium, pytest, Pydantic, and Windows workflow. |
| Browser automation | Selenium | 4.43.0 | Industry-standard WebDriver automation; Selenium Manager reduces driver mismatch issues. |
| Test runner | pytest | 9.0.3 | Mature, readable, parameterized testing support. |
| HTML report | pytest-html | 4.2.0 | Generates academic/professional HTML test reports. |
| Coverage | pytest-cov | 7.1.0 | Measures unit-test coverage. |
| HTML parsing | beautifulsoup4 | 4.14.3 | Stable HTML parsing for form metadata extraction. |
| Data validation | Pydantic | 2.13.3 | Strict schema validation for generated/reviewed/final JSON test cases. |
| LLM runtime | Ollama | 0.23.2 or newer | Local LLM execution; avoids paid APIs and protects project reproducibility. |
| LLM model | llama3.2:3b | pinned by Ollama tag | Small enough for normal laptops; good enough for structured SQA generation. |
| HTTP client | requests | 2.33.1 | Stable HTTP client for Ollama and health checks. |
| Env config | python-dotenv | 1.2.2 | Loads local `.env` configuration safely. |
| Code quality | Ruff | 0.15.12 | Fast linting and formatting. |
| Type checking | mypy | 1.20.0 | Static checking for professional code quality. |

---

## 2. Hardware and OS Requirements

Minimum local setup:

- OS: Windows 10 64-bit or Windows 11 64-bit
- RAM: 8 GB minimum, 16 GB recommended
- Disk: 10 GB free minimum
- CPU: 4-core recommended
- Browser: Google Chrome stable
- Internet: required because target forms are public websites

For Ollama + `llama3.2:3b`:

- RAM: 8 GB minimum
- Model download size: about 2 GB
- GPU: optional, not required

---

## 3. Required Software Installation Order

Install in this order to avoid path and dependency issues:

1. Google Chrome stable
2. Python 3.13.13
3. Git for Windows 2.54.0
4. Visual Studio Code latest stable
5. Ollama 0.23.2 or newer
6. Postman or Insomnia
7. Project dependencies inside a Python virtual environment

---

## 4. Windows Installation Steps

### 4.1 Install Google Chrome

Download Chrome from the official Google Chrome page. After installation, verify:

```powershell
chrome --version
```

If PowerShell says `chrome` is not recognized, open Chrome manually and go to:

```text
chrome://version
```

The framework does not require manual ChromeDriver installation. Selenium Manager handles driver discovery and download automatically.

### 4.2 Install Python 3.13.13

Download Python 3.13.13 from the official Python downloads page.

During installation:

- Tick `Add python.exe to PATH`
- Select `Install Now`
- After installation, open a new PowerShell window

Verify:

```powershell
python --version
pip --version
```

Expected:

```text
Python 3.13.13
pip 25.x or newer
```

If `python` opens Microsoft Store, disable App Execution Aliases:

```text
Windows Settings > Apps > Advanced app settings > App execution aliases > turn off python.exe and python3.exe
```

### 4.3 Install Git for Windows 2.54.0

Download Git from the official Git for Windows page.

Recommended installer choices:

- Default editor: Visual Studio Code
- PATH option: Git from command line and third-party software
- HTTPS transport: OpenSSL
- Line endings: Checkout Windows-style, commit Unix-style

Verify:

```powershell
git --version
```

Expected:

```text
git version 2.54.0.windows.1
```

### 4.4 Install Visual Studio Code

Install VS Code latest stable. Add these extensions:

- Python by Microsoft
- Pylance
- Ruff
- GitLens
- YAML

Verify VS Code can open from terminal:

```powershell
code --version
```

### 4.5 Install Ollama

Install Ollama for Windows. Then open PowerShell:

```powershell
ollama --version
ollama pull llama3.2:3b
ollama list
```

Expected model list includes:

```text
llama3.2:3b
```

Check the API:

```powershell
curl http://localhost:11434/api/tags
```

Expected: JSON response with available models.

---

## 5. Project Setup

### 5.1 Create project folder

```powershell
mkdir C:\sqa-projects
cd C:\sqa-projects
```

Extract this project ZIP into:

```text
C:\sqa-projects\llm-sqa-form-tester
```

Then enter the project:

```powershell
cd C:\sqa-projects\llm-sqa-form-tester
```

You may run the included setup script instead of typing the setup commands manually:

```powershell
.\scripts\setup_windows.ps1
```

### 5.2 Create virtual environment with Python 3.13

Use the Windows Python launcher so the environment is definitely created with Python 3.13, even if another Python version is also installed.

```powershell
py -3.13 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python --version
python -m pip install --upgrade pip==25.1.1 setuptools==80.9.0 wheel==0.45.1
```

Expected Python version:

```text
Python 3.13.x
```

If PowerShell blocks activation:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

### 5.3 Install dependencies and local package

```powershell
pip install -r requirements.txt
pip install -e .
```

The `pip install -e .` command is required because the project uses a `src/` package layout. Without it, Python may raise `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'llm_sqa'`.

Copy the environment file if it does not exist:

```powershell
copy .env.example .env
```

Verify:

```powershell
python -m llm_sqa.cli doctor
```

Expected: JSON output with `python_ok: true`, `imports_ok: true`, and browser/session checks.

---

## 6. Configuration

Copy environment example:

```powershell
copy .env.example .env
```

Default `.env`:

```env
OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2:3b
HEADLESS=true
DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
DATA_DIR=data
REPORT_DIR=reports
```

Set `HEADLESS=false` if you want to watch the browser.

---

## 7. Complete Development Workflow

Run the full pipeline:

```powershell
python -m llm_sqa.cli all
```

This performs:

1. Extract metadata
2. Generate test cases
3. Review/filter cases
4. Enrich cases with severity and expected result
5. Save final approved test cases

Run tests:

```powershell
pytest
```

Run only unit tests:

```powershell
pytest tests/unit -q
```

Run only browser tests:

```powershell
pytest tests/e2e -q --html=reports/e2e_report.html --self-contained-html
```

Generate coverage:

```powershell
pytest tests/unit --cov=src/llm_sqa --cov-report=term-missing --cov-report=html:reports/coverage
```

Lint and format:

```powershell
ruff check .
ruff format .
```

Type-check:

```powershell
mypy src
```

---

## 8. Artifact Outputs

The framework creates:

```text
data/metadata/*.json
data/test_cases/generated_cases.json
data/test_cases/reviewed_cases.json
data/test_cases/rejected_cases.json
data/test_cases/final_test_cases.json
reports/e2e_report.html
reports/coverage/index.html
logs/app.log
```

These files are useful for academic submission because they show traceability from extraction to execution.

